1. Bootstrap Grid System vs Plain CSS ?
Bootstrap uses a 12-column grid system to easily divide page layout.
Provides predefined classes like col-md-6, col-lg-4 instead of writing CSS manually.
Automatically handles spacing, alignment, and responsiveness.
Reduces need for writing complex media queries.
Faster development compared to plain CSS where layout must be built from scratch.


2. Bootstrap Components (Navbar & Cards) ?
Ready-made components like navbar, cards, buttons are pre-designed.
No need to code UI from scratch → saves time.
Ensures consistent design across the website.
Built-in responsiveness and styling.
Easily customizable using classes.


3. Responsive Utility Classes & Breakpoints ?
Bootstrap provides breakpoints (sm, md, lg, xl, xxl).
Layout adjusts automatically based on screen size.
Utility classes like:
d-none d-md-block (hide/show elements)
text-center text-lg-start
Ensures website works on mobile, tablet, and desktop.
Eliminates need for manual responsive CSS.


4. Form Validation & User Trust ?
Shows real-time feedback (valid/invalid input).
Prevents submission of incorrect data.
Improves user experience.
Makes website look secure and professional.
Builds trust because users know their data is handled properly.


5. Utility Classes Reduce Repetitive CSS ?
Bootstrap provides classes like:
mt-3 (margin-top)
p-2 (padding)
text-center
No need to write custom CSS repeatedly.
Faster styling with single-line classes.
Keeps code clean and maintainable.


6. Converting Page to React or Angular ?
Bootstrap can be used in React/Angular easily.
HTML structure can be converted into components.
Use libraries like:
React-Bootstrap
NG Bootstrap (Angular)
Reusable components improve maintainability.
Dynamic data can be added using state/props (React) or bindings (Angular).